N1  - Funding text 1: The Gulf Coast region extending from South Texas to Florida serves as the testbed for the TBRIC implementation in this study. Given the similarities in hazard experiences in the past two decades with repeated land-falling tropical storms and flooding in both urban and rural locations, significant property and crop losses, and demographic changes, this region has been ground zero for understanding the variability in resilience capacity. The authors also have considerable field experience working in this region [31], understanding resilience-focused longitudinal datasets covering the region [32], and participating in collaborative projects sponsored by the Gulf Research Program of the United States’ National Academies of Science, Engineering, and Medicine (NASEM).
N1  - Funding text 2: Funding: This research was funded by the Gulf Research Program of the National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine (Grant Agreement number 20001088, “The New First Line of Defense: Building Community Resilience through Residential Risk Disclosure”). Any opinions, findings, conclusions, and recommendations expressed in this paper are those of the authors and do not necessarily reflect the official policy or position of the funders.
N1  - Funding text 3: Acknowledgments: The authors acknowledge the support from the Hazards Vulnerability and Resilience Institute (HVRI), University of South Carolina colleagues, the Brazilian Fulbright Commission for Julia F. Effgen’s research award, and are thankful for the contributions by Rachel Reeves McCaster (South Carolina Emergency Management Division) and Erika Pham (Texas A&M AgriLife Extension).
