N1  - Funding text 1: Clear doctor patient communication is integral to good clinical care and considered a core competency of medical training. However, communication skills training is cost and time intensive [1] . Technology-based approaches offer the capability of replicating clinical environments and providing feedback, while reducing training resources. These approaches range in complexity from use of telehealth video platforms to engage with remote simulated patients [2] to avatars who engage in virtual human simulation (e.g. Kognito© or AliveSim© platforms) [3,4] . Other innovative communication skills technology uses include interactive web-based video simulations [5] , a web-based video annotation system to support student self-evaluation [6] , and the recently developed Video-Based Communication Assessment supported by the National Board of Medical Examiners [7] . While these approaches are authentic to the clinical environment, they also are time-intensive to build and use, costly in effort, and may require use of computer software or hardware at a central site, limiting portability.
N1  - Funding text 2: This project was supported by the Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality under grant number R18HS025651 . The content is solely the responsibility of the authors and does not necessarily represent the official views of the Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality.
