N1  - Funding text 1: This work was fully funded by a PhD scholarship by the University “G. d’Annunzio” of Chieti-Pescara (Italy) and was carried out in association with the “Center for Risk-Based Community Resilience Planning - A NIST-funded Center of Excellence” at the Colorado State University, Fort Collins, USA.
N1  - Funding text 2: Planning for disasters may include, among other actions, the drafting of plans or checklists aimed at quantifying and monitoring the hospital preparedness. Considering the surge of patients caused by the ongoing SARS-CoV-2 outbreak [9] and realizing the need to be better prepared for future pandemics, the World Health Organization has developed specific tools for policy makers and planners of all Member States to preventively check the healthcare bed, staff and supply capacity [10]. Despite being crucial, planning for disasters (especially those with low occurrence probability such as major earthquakes) is often deemed difficult and expensive to achieve because of the already high demand for ordinary healthcare services that take priority [11]. The United States [12] and the European Union [13] require and support the adoption of disaster preparedness plans for healthcare systems. In the specific case of Italy, all public or private hospitals with an Emergency Department (ED) are required by law to adopt a so-called PEIMAF (Internal Emergency Plan in case of Massive Influx of Patients) [14]. The PEIMAF must address all possible environmental, man-made and NBCR (Nuclear, Biological, Chemical and Radioactive) hazards. Preventive drills are key to test the efficacy of PEIMAFs and to increase the hospital response capacity, but they are expensive [15], time-consuming to plan, and may suffer from a lack of realism [16].
