TI  - Trust-oriented affordances: A five-country study of news trustworthiness and its socio-technical articulations
T2  - New Media and Society
J2  - New Media and Society
PY  - 2022
DO  - 10.1177/14614448221096334
SN  - 14614448 (ISSN) 
AU  - Aharoni, T.
AU  - Tenenboim-Weinblatt, K.
AU  - Kligler-Vilenchik, N.
AU  - Boczkowski, P.
AU  - Hayashi, K.
AU  - Mitchelstein, E.
AU  - Villi, M.
AD  - Hebrew University of Jerusalem, Israel
AD  - Northwestern University, United States
AD  - The University of Tokyo, Japan
AD  - Universidad de San Andrés, Argentina
AD  - University of Jyväskylä, Finland
AB  - Research on trust has come to the forefront of communication studies. Beyond the dominant focus on informational trust and its country-specific articulations, trustworthiness evaluations can relate to the materiality of news and its global manifestations. Especially in digital algorithmic environments, understanding news trustworthiness requires a holistic approach, which combines informational and socio-technical aspects while addressing both institutional and interpersonal trust. Drawing on 488 in-depth interviews with media consumers in Argentina, Finland, Israel, Japan, and the United States, this article investigates news (dis)trust from the lens of socio-materiality. The six trust-oriented affordances we identified—selectivity, interactivity, customization, searchability, information abundance, and immediacy—reveal important socio-technical commonalities that underlie news trust across countries. These affordances, moreover, point to an interplay of trust and self-agency. Taken together, the findings illuminate the lived experience of news trust as manifested across cultures and offer a broader understanding of trustworthiness within current media ecology. © The Author(s) 2022.
