N1  - Funding text 1: This work was supported by the Horizon 2020 [788204]. This work was supported by the European Union Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme under Grant [No 788204]. The authors thank the ACT on Gender project consortium and members from which this paper emerged and acknowledge their support.
N1  - Funding text 2: The first EU-wide investment to raise awareness and enhance gender competences among researchers and research support officers was the Gender in Research project that ran from 2010 to 2012 (Sangiuliano & Cortesi, , p. 180). Since then, various strategies for achieving GE in institutions have been implemented ranging from gender mainstreaming, gender impact assessments, gender budgeting, and gender equality plans (GEPs) (Ikävalko & Kantola, ). GEPs focus on policy interventions within institutions, and they are structured into the stages of analysis, planning, implementation, and monitoring (European Comission, ). However, GEPs have not been immune to criticism in relation to efficiency, efficacy, or failure to recognize the complexity of structural, cultural, and institutional factors that impede women in HE and research and innovation (R&I) (Kalpazidou Schmidt & Cacace, ). Further, it is argued that the work of GEPs falls mainly on those it purports to help, and that they are dealt with as a tick-box exercise, undermining the possibility for radical change (Caffrey et al., ). To an extent, this was addressed in funded coordination and support action projects (for example, European Commission Calls in Horizon 2020 Gender in Research and Innovation), where applicants applied for funding to implement GEPs within their own partner institutions, and were the core beneficiaries of the funds and outputs. These projects had to be signed off by the main bodies of governance of HE or research institutions. Consequently, building a GEP was a goal for the whole institution, and it provided the strategic purpose and validation of GE work.
